  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Recycling for the Environment to Strengthen Income and Livelihood of the Entrepreneurs (RESILIENT)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhance the livelihood of the waste actors by strengthening the value chain to increase collection of plastic waste by improving waste governance in land and water bodies in Dhaka North and Narayanganj.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In response to the escalating global plastic waste crisis, Coca-Cola and Cordaid launched a pioneering project in Bangladesh aimed at mitigating the environmental impact of plastic pollution. Focusing on the Dhaka North City Corporation (DNCC) and Narayanganj City Corporation (NCC), the initiative sought to empower informal waste collectors and aggregators for sustainable plastic waste management. Despite encountering challenges in aligning with the DNCC&apos;s Waste to Energy focus, the project excelled in Narayanganj, where the city&apos;s emphasis on source segregation provided a conducive environment. The project, culminating in the successful removal of over 1200 tons of plastic waste, received accolades from government officials, and stakeholders.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project targets 500 waste collectors (40% female) and aggregators, providing training in entrepreneurship, market linkage, and occupational health and safety. In Dhaka, focused efforts will be concentrated in two key clusters, namely Korail and Kalshi, involving 100 waste collectors and 10 waste aggregators. Simultaneously, Narayanganj will see interventions in five clusters, including Jimkhana, Paikpara, Chandmari, Muslimnagar, and Chashara, engaging a larger workforce of 400 waste collectors and 20 waste aggregators. In total, the project will operate across seven clusters, aiming to empower and build the capacity of 500 waste collectors, with a particular emphasis on promoting entrepreneurship and ensuring occupational safety. Furthermore, 30 waste aggregators in these areas will receive targeted support for business processes and enterprise promotion, contributing to a comprehensive and sustainable waste management approach.</narrative>
  </description>
